
---
import InnerLayout from "../../layouts/InnerLayout.astro";

const articleTitle = "Where to Deploy AI-Built Apps: An Honest Comparison";
const articleDescription =
  "Compare Render, Railway, Fly.io, Vercel, and Nixora for deploying AI-built apps. Explore pricing, trade-offs, setup requirements, and practical deployment advice.";

const articleUrl =
  "https://nixora.co.in/resources/where-to-deploy-ai-built-apps/";

const tableOfContents = [
  { id: "short-answer", title: "The Short Answer" },
  { id: "why-different", title: "Why AI-Built Apps Are Different" },
  { id: "what-to-compare", title: "What to Compare Before You Pick" },
  { id: "comparison", title: "Side-by-Side Comparison" },
  { id: "render", title: "Render" },
  { id: "railway", title: "Railway" },
  { id: "flyio", title: "Fly.io" },
  { id: "vercel", title: "Vercel" },
  { id: "nixora", title: "Nixora" },
  { id: "decision-framework", title: "How to Choose" },
  { id: "hidden-costs", title: "Hidden Costs" },
  { id: "pre-deploy-checklist", title: "Pre-Deploy Checklist" },
  { id: "agent-deploy", title: "Letting an AI Agent Deploy" },
  { id: "common-mistakes", title: "Common Mistakes" },
  { id: "faq", title: "Frequently Asked Questions" },
  { id: "pick-and-ship", title: "Pick One and Ship It This Week" },
];

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    platform: "Render",
    bestFor: "Backends with managed databases",
    free: "Free web service that sleeps after 15 minutes",
    paid: "$7/month per always-on service",
    pricing: "Fixed price per service",
    drawback: "Costs stack per service",
  },
  {
    platform: "Railway",
    bestFor: "Fast setup, small backends",
    free: "One-time $5 trial credit",
    paid: "$5/month (Hobby)",
    pricing: "Metered usage",
    drawback: "Bill can drift with usage",
  },
  {
    platform: "Fly.io",
    bestFor: "Multi-region, container control",
    free: "Short trial only",
    paid: "Pay as you go",
    pricing: "Per-second metering",
    drawback: "Steeper learning curve",
  },
  {
    platform: "Vercel",
    bestFor: "Next.js and frontends",
    free: "Hobby, non-commercial only",
    paid: "$20/user/month (Pro)",
    pricing: "Seat plus usage",
    drawback: "Not built for long-running servers",
  },
  {
    platform: "Nixora",
    bestFor: "Deploying AI-built apps with failure diagnosis",
    free: "None, access is closed",
    paid: "Not yet public",
    pricing: "Not yet public",
    drawback: "Early, CLI access closed",
  },
];

const platformSections = [
  {
    id: "render",
    title: "Render: The Predictable Option",
    paragraphs: [
      "Render is the closest thing to a classic platform-as-a-service that still feels modern. Connect a Git repo, set a build command and a start command, and it deploys on every push. Managed Postgres and Redis live in the same dashboard, and pull requests get preview environments.",
      "The pricing is easy to reason about because each service has its own price. An always-on web service starts at $7 a month. The free tier exists, but free web services spin down after 15 minutes without traffic and take about a minute to wake up. Free Postgres databases also expire after a limited period. That is fine for a demo and bad for anything a customer touches.",
    ],
    drawbackTitle: "Where it bites",
    drawback:
      "Costs add up per service. A web service, a database, and a background worker are 3 line items, not 1. Teams budget for the first and forget the rest.",
    take:
      "Render is the safest default for a standard backend with a database. It is not the cheapest and it is not the most flexible, but it is the one I would least expect to surprise you.",
  },
  {
    id: "railway",
    title: "Railway: The Fastest First Deploy",
    paragraphs: [
      "Railway’s pitch is speed. Point it at a repo and it figures out most of the setup. Databases take a couple of clicks. For AI-built apps, that matters, because the code often lacks the config that stricter platforms expect.",
      "Pricing is metered. Hobby is $5 a month and includes $5 of usage. Pro is $20 a month and includes $20. You pay for CPU and memory by the second, so a quiet app costs little and a busy one costs more. New accounts get a one-time trial credit of $5.",
    ],
    drawbackTitle: "Where it bites",
    drawback:
      "Metered billing is cheap until it isn’t. An app with a memory leak or a runaway loop gets expensive quietly, and the pricing page does not prepare you for that. Set usage alerts on day 1.",
    take:
      "Railway is where I would start a side project or an early backend. Move off it only if the bill gets weird or you need something it does not offer.",
  },
  {
    id: "flyio",
    title: "Fly.io: The Most Control",
    paragraphs: [
      "Fly.io runs your app as Machines, small VMs that boot from a container image, and lets you place them in regions close to your users. If you need low latency in several countries, or long-lived connections like WebSockets, this is where it shines.",
      "There is no ongoing free tier for new accounts. You get a short trial, then pay by the second for what runs. A small always-on machine costs a few dollars a month, but the total depends on how you configure it, and the bill has several meters: compute, storage, IP addresses, and traffic.",
    ],
    drawbackTitle: "Where it bites",
    drawback:
      "The learning curve. You will work with a CLI, a config file, and a container mindset. If your app has no Dockerfile, expect extra work. Managed Postgres also costs more than the metered databases on Railway, so compare before you commit.",
    take:
      "Fly.io is great when you know why you need it. For the first deploy of an AI-built prototype, it is usually more platform than you need.",
  },
  {
    id: "vercel",
    title: "Vercel: Best for Frontends",
    paragraphs: [
      "Vercel is built around the frontend, and especially Next.js. Push to Git, get a preview URL for every branch, and a global CDN without thinking about it. For AI-generated React and Next.js apps, it is hard to beat.",
      "The Hobby plan is free but limited to personal, non-commercial projects. Anything with revenue needs Pro, which is $20 per user per month with $20 of usage credit, plus metered usage beyond that.",
    ],
    drawbackTitle: "Where it bites",
    drawback:
      "The backend. Vercel runs your server code as functions, not as a persistent process. An Express or Django server that expects to run all day fits awkwardly. Bandwidth and function usage are metered too, so a traffic spike raises the bill. Turn on spend limits if your plan has them.",
    take:
      "Use Vercel for the frontend and put the backend somewhere else. Splitting them is common and works well.",
  },
  {
    id: "nixora",
    title: "Nixora: The Early Option Built for This Problem",
    paragraphs: [
      "Nixora is the one I work on, so read this section with that in mind. It is a control plane for AI-generated software and AI agents, and today it covers 2 things: deploying an app and explaining why a deployment failed.",
      "The workflow is 3 commands. You install the CLI with pip install nixora, run nixora init, then run nixora deploy. It detects the runtime, builds a container without a Dockerfile, provisions compute, and issues HTTPS. If a deploy fails, it tells you what went wrong and what to change.",
      "Here is the honest part. CLI access is closed right now while the team hardens the infrastructure. An MCP server for agents, per-agent permissions, and audit trails are on the roadmap, not shipped. There is no public pricing yet. So it is not a production choice today, and I won’t pretend otherwise.",
      "Why mention it at all? Because the gap it targets is real. Most platforms assume you understand your stack. AI-built apps often leave you without that understanding, so a clear explanation of a failure matters as much as the deploy itself.",
    ],
    drawbackTitle: "My take",
    drawback:
      "If you build with AI tools and fight deploy errors, join the waitlist at nixora.co.in. For anything shipping to customers this month, use one of the 4 above.",
  },
];

const decisionPoints = [
  {
    title: "You built a Next.js or React frontend.",
    text: "Use Vercel. Add a separate backend if you need one.",
  },
  {
    title: "You built a backend with a database and want the least friction.",
    text: "Railway if you hate setup, Render if you hate surprise bills.",
  },
  {
    title: "You need users in several regions, or long-lived connections.",
    text: "Use Fly.io.",
  },
  {
    title: "You need always-on with no cold starts.",
    text: "Use a paid tier on Render or Railway, or run Machines on Fly.io. Skip any free tier that sleeps.",
  },
  {
    title: "You are building a prototype to show 5 people.",
    text: "A free Render service is fine. Accept the wake-up delay and tell your viewers to wait a minute.",
  },
  {
    title: "You build with AI agents and keep fighting deploy errors.",
    text: "Join the Nixora waitlist, and use Railway or Render in the meantime.",
  },
];

const hiddenCosts = [
  {
    title: "Supporting services.",
    text: "The web service is rarely the whole bill. A database, a cache, and a worker each add their own line. Price the full stack, not just the app.",
  },
  {
    title: "Free tier expiry.",
    text: "Free databases can expire and free services can sleep. Plenty of people find out the day a demo breaks.",
  },
  {
    title: "Seats.",
    text: "Some plans charge per user. A $20 plan for 1 person becomes $100 for 5.",
  },
  {
    title: "Traffic.",
    text: "Bandwidth is metered on several platforms. A post that goes well can raise your bill before you notice. Set an alert or a spend cap before you share a link.",
  },
  {
    title: "Your time.",
    text: "This is the biggest one. If a platform saves $10 a month and costs you 6 hours of debugging, it is not cheaper. Value your afternoons.",
  },
];

const checklist = [
  {
    title: "Read the port from the environment.",
    text: "Use the PORT variable, not a hardcoded 3000. This single fix solves a huge share of first-deploy timeouts.",
  },
  {
    title: "Pin the runtime.",
    text: "Say which Node or Python version you use. Unpinned versions break builds the week a new release lands.",
  },
  {
    title: "Move secrets to environment variables.",
    text: "No keys in files, and none in git. If one was ever committed, rotate it.",
  },
  {
    title: "Make one start command work.",
    text: "You should be able to start the app with a single command on a clean machine. If you cannot, the platform cannot either.",
  },
  {
    title: "Add a health endpoint.",
    text: "A simple route that returns 200 lets the platform tell if your app is alive. Better, have it check the database connection.",
  },
  {
    title: "Check the database URL.",
    text: "Make sure the app reads it from an environment variable and not from a local file.",
  },
  {
    title: "Run the build locally.",
    text: "If the build fails on your machine, it will fail on theirs. Read the output before you push.",
  },
];

const commonMistakes = [
  {
    title: "Choosing by hype.",
    text: "The platform everyone tweets about may not fit a plain backend. Pick by the shape of your app.",
  },
  {
    title: "Running production on a free tier.",
    text: "Sleeping services and expiring databases are built for testing. Treat them that way.",
  },
  {
    title: "Putting everything in one place.",
    text: "A Vercel frontend with a Railway backend is a perfectly good setup. One platform does not need to do it all.",
  },
  {
    title: "Ignoring the exit.",
    text: "If you cannot move your app in a day, you are locked in. Keep config in your repo and stay close to standard containers.",
  },
  {
    title: "Skipping the rollback test.",
    text: "Deploy a broken change to a test environment on purpose, then roll it back. A rollback you have never practiced is a hope, not a plan.",
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "What is the easiest way to deploy an AI-built app?",
    answer:
      "Use a platform that reads your setup from a Git repo, like Railway or Render. Fix the basics first: read the port from an environment variable, pin your runtime, and keep secrets out of code. Most failed first deploys come from those 3 things, not from the platform.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I deploy an AI-built app for free?",
    answer:
      "Yes, with limits. Render has a free web service that sleeps after 15 minutes idle. Vercel’s Hobby plan is free for non-commercial projects. Railway gives a small trial credit, and Fly.io offers only a short trial. Free tiers suit demos and are risky for anything customers use.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is Railway better than Render?",
    answer:
      "Neither wins outright. Railway is faster to set up and bills by usage, so small apps are cheap and busy ones can surprise you. Render charges a fixed price per service, which is easier to predict but adds up. Pick Railway for speed and Render for predictability.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is Vercel good for backend apps?",
    answer:
      "It depends on the backend. Vercel runs server code as functions, which suits APIs attached to a frontend. It fits poorly for long-running servers, background workers, or WebSockets. For those, use Railway, Render, or Fly.io and keep Vercel for the frontend.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need Docker to deploy an app?",
    answer:
      "No. Render and Railway can build from source, and tools like Nixora aim to containerize for you. Fly.io works best with a container image. Docker helps if you want to move between platforms, but your first deploy does not need it.",
  },
];

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  mainEntityOfPage: {
    "@type": "WebPage",
    "@id": articleUrl,
  },
  headline: articleTitle,
  description: articleDescription,
  author: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "Nixora",
    url: "https://nixora.co.in/",
  },
  publisher: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "Nixora",
    url: "https://nixora.co.in/",
    logo: {
      "@type": "ImageObject",
      url: "https://nixora.co.in/logo-full.jpg",
    },
  },
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      position: 1,
      name: "Home",
      item: "https://nixora.co.in/",
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      position: 2,
      name: "Resources",
      item: "https://nixora.co.in/resources/",
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      position: 3,
      name: articleTitle,
      item: articleUrl,
    },
  ],
};
---

<InnerLayout
  title={`${articleTitle} | Nixora`}
  description={articleDescription}
  jsonLd={[articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <main class="article-page">
    <!-- Article header -->
    <header class="article-header">
      <a href="/resources" class="back-link">
        <span aria-hidden="true" class="back-arrow">←</span>
        Back to Resources
      </a>

      <div class="article-meta">
        <span class="article-category">AI Infrastructure</span>
        <span class="article-date">November 2026</span>
        <span class="meta-dot"></span>
        <span class="article-read-time">10 min read</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="article-title">{articleTitle}</h1>

      <p class="article-intro">
        You built the app in an afternoon. Cursor or Claude Code wrote most of
        it. It runs perfectly on localhost:3000. Now you need a URL other
        people can open, and that is where the afternoon turns into a week.
      </p>

      <div class="author">
        <div class="author-avatar">N</div>
        <div class="author-info">
          <p class="author-name">Nixora</p>
          <p class="author-description">AI-native infrastructure</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Article content and table of contents -->
    <div class="article-layout">
      <!-- Table of contents -->
      <aside class="article-sidebar">
        <div class="toc-sticky">
          <p class="toc-label">On this page</p>

          <nav aria-label="Table of contents" class="toc-nav">
            <ul class="toc-list">
              {tableOfContents.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={`#${item.id}`} class="toc-link">
                    {item.title}
                  </a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </nav>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <!-- Full article -->
      <article class="article-content">
        <!-- Introduction -->
        <div class="article-introduction">
          <p class="article-paragraph">
            Where you deploy matters more than it used to. AI-generated code
            makes quiet assumptions about ports, runtimes, and databases, and
            every platform handles those gaps differently. Some forgive them.
            Some punish them.
          </p>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
            This guide compares 5 options for deploying AI-built apps: Render,
            Railway, Fly.io, Vercel, and Nixora. You get real pricing, honest
            weak spots, a decision framework, and a pre-deploy checklist you
            can use on any of them.
          </p>

          <div class="disclosure">
            <p class="article-paragraph">
              A disclosure first. I work on Nixora, so I have a stake in this
              comparison. I’ve tried to be fair, and Nixora is the only option
              here that I’ll tell you not to use for production yet.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- The Short Answer -->
        <section id="short-answer" class="article-section">
          <div class="section-heading-wrap">
            <h2 class="section-heading">The Short Answer</h2>
            <div class="section-divider"></div>
          </div>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
            For a Next.js or frontend-heavy app, deploy on Vercel. For a backend
            with a database and the least setup, pick Railway or Render. For
            multi-region control, choose Fly.io. If you build with AI agents
            and want deployment failures explained, watch Nixora, though it is
            early.
          </p>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
            The rest of this guide explains why, and where each one bites.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Why AI-built apps are different -->
        <section id="why-different" class="article-section">
          <div class="section-heading-wrap">
            <h2 class="section-heading">Why Deploying AI-Built Apps Is Different</h2>
            <div class="section-divider"></div>
          </div>

          <div class="article-subsection">
            <h3 class="subsection-heading">The Code Assumes Your Laptop</h3>
            <p class="article-paragraph">
              AI tools write code that works where it was written. That means
              hardcoded ports, a database URL sitting in a local file, a start
              command that only exists in the chat history, and a runtime
              version nobody pinned. A person who wrote the app by hand would
              remember these details. Here, nobody wrote it, so nobody
              remembers them.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="article-subsection">
            <h3 class="subsection-heading">Nobody Knows the Stack Well</h3>
            <p class="article-paragraph">
              When you build by hand, you know why every piece is there. When
              an agent builds it, you often know what the app does but not how
              it is wired. That makes deploy errors harder to read. A build
              log that says “module not found” is a puzzle when you have never
              opened the file it points to.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="article-subsection">
            <h3 class="subsection-heading">The Agent May Be the One Deploying</h3>
            <p class="article-paragraph">
              More teams now let the coding agent run the deploy command
              itself. That adds new criteria. Along with price and speed, you
              care about what a token can touch and whether anyone can see
              what happened afterward. That is a bigger topic, but keep it in
              mind as you read.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- What to compare -->
        <section id="what-to-compare" class="article-section">
          <div class="section-heading-wrap">
            <h2 class="section-heading">What to Compare Before You Pick Anything</h2>
            <div class="section-divider"></div>
          </div>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
            Ignore feature lists. Six questions decide most of the outcome.
          </p>

          <div class="comparison-questions">
            <p class="article-paragraph">
              <strong>How much setup does it take?</strong>
              {" "}Some platforms read your repo and figure it out. Others want
              a Dockerfile and a config file. AI-built apps usually lack both,
              so this matters more than it sounds.
            </p>

            <p class="article-paragraph">
              <strong>What does the price do as you grow?</strong>
              {" "}Fixed prices per service are easy to predict. Metered prices
              are cheap while you are small and harder to forecast later.
              Neither is better. They just fail differently.
            </p>

            <p class="article-paragraph">
              <strong>Does it sleep?</strong>
              {" "}Free tiers often stop your app after a quiet period. The
              next visitor waits while it wakes up. That is fine for a demo
              and bad for a product.
            </p>

            <p class="article-paragraph">
              <strong>How easy are databases?</strong>
              {" "}Most apps need one within a week. A platform with a database
              one click away saves you a day.
            </p>

            <p class="article-paragraph">
              <strong>What happens when a deploy fails?</strong>
              {" "}Some platforms hand you a raw log. Others help you read it.
              For apps you did not write by hand, that gap is huge.
            </p>

            <p class="article-paragraph">
              <strong>What can a token or an agent touch?</strong>
              {" "}If an agent will deploy for you, look at how access is
              scoped before you hand over a key.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Comparison table -->
        <section id="comparison" class="article-section">
          <div class="section-heading-wrap">
            <h2 class="section-heading">Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>
            <div class="section-divider"></div>
          </div>

          <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
            <table class="comparison-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  {["Platform", "Best for", "Free option", "Paid entry point", "Pricing shape", "Where it bites"].map((heading) => (
                    <th>{heading}</th>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {comparisonRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="platform-name">{row.platform}</td>
                    <td>{row.bestFor}</td>
                    <td>{row.free}</td>
                    <td>{row.paid}</td>
                    <td>{row.pricing}</td>
                    <td>{row.drawback}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p class="table-note">
            Prices were checked in November 2026. They change often, so
            confirm on each platform’s pricing page before you commit.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Individual platform sections -->
        {platformSections.map((platform) => (
          <section id={platform.id} class="article-section">
            <div class="section-heading-wrap">
              <h2 class="section-heading">{platform.title}</h2>
              <div class="section-divider"></div>
            </div>

            <div class="platform-paragraphs">
              {platform.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
                <p class="article-paragraph">{paragraph}</p>
              ))}
            </div>

            <div class="article-subsection">
              <h3 class="subsection-heading">{platform.drawbackTitle}</h3>
              <p class="article-paragraph">{platform.drawback}</p>
            </div>

            {platform.take && (
              <div class="takeaway">
                <p class="article-paragraph">
                  <strong>My take: </strong>
                  {platform.take}
                </p>
              </div>
            )}
          </section>
        ))}

        <!-- Decision framework -->
        <section id="decision-framework" class="article-section">
          <div class="section-heading-wrap">
            <h2 class="section-heading">How to Choose: A Decision Framework</h2>
            <div class="section-divider"></div>
          </div>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
            Match the platform to the app, not the other way around.
          </p>

          <div class="decision-list">
            {decisionPoints.map((point) => (
              <div class="decision-item">
                <h3 class="decision-title">{point.title}</h3>
                <p class="article-paragraph">{point.text}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
            If 2 options fit, pick the one with the cheaper exit. Moving a
            containerized app between these platforms takes hours. Moving one
            that leans hard on a single platform’s features can take weeks.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Hidden costs -->
        <section id="hidden-costs" class="article-section">
          <div class="section-heading-wrap">
            <h2 class="section-heading">The Hidden Costs Nobody Puts on the Pricing Page</h2>
            <div class="section-divider"></div>
          </div>

          <div class="cost-list">
            {hiddenCosts.map((item) => (
              <p class="article-paragraph">
                <strong>{item.title}</strong>
                {" "}{item.text}
              </p>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Pre-deploy checklist -->
        <section id="pre-deploy-checklist" class="article-section">
          <div class="section-heading-wrap">
            <h2 class="section-heading">A Pre-Deploy Checklist for AI-Generated Code</h2>
            <div class="section-divider"></div>
          </div>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
            Do this before you touch any platform. It fixes most deploy
            failures before they happen.
          </p>

          <div class="checklist">
            {checklist.map((item, index) => (
              <div class="checklist-item">
                <span class="checklist-number">{index + 1}</span>
                <div class="checklist-content">
                  <h3 class="checklist-title">{item.title}</h3>
                  <p class="article-paragraph">{item.text}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- AI agent deployment -->
        <section id="agent-deploy" class="article-section">
          <div class="section-heading-wrap">
            <h2 class="section-heading">What About Letting an AI Agent Do the Deploy?</h2>
            <div class="section-divider"></div>
          </div>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
            It is tempting. The agent wrote the code, so why not let it ship
            it? Most of these platforms have a CLI or an API an agent can call.
          </p>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
            My view: yes, with limits. Give the agent a token that can deploy
            1 service to a staging environment, not an account-wide key. Keep
            production behind a human approval. Make sure you can see what the
            agent did afterward, and make sure you can roll back in 1 step.
          </p>

          <p class="article-paragraph">
